-<section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Slowing the clock is not a free adjustment.</h2><p>For each physical canonical pair, normalized plant coefficients satisfy αβ=t₀²/(LC). Their smallest possible common maximum is t₀/√(LC), achieved by balanced flux–charge scales. The inherited λ≤1 requirement therefore needs t₀≤min √(LC).</p><p>The timing allocation gives the other side: t₀ must be at least 400000 times the largest of output period, filter-history moment plus electronics latency, and actuator time constant. Both sides must hold on calibrated intervals.</p><p>The audited averaging-mode spacing yields a lower clock of 2048/5 seconds under this allocation. Every pair would then require LC≥4194304/25 seconds squared. A declared LC≤1 second squared envelope has no overlap. This is a restriction on the stated contract, not a universal impossibility result.</p></div></section>
+<aside class="callout"><strong>Next milestone: a preparation-specific feasible clock interval</strong><p>A local-box proof now admits a fast-acquisition route under new calibrated premises. The earlier full-box timing restriction retains its scope.</p><a href="/evidence/prefix-feasibility/">Read the local-prefix feasibility theorem →</a></aside><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Slowing the clock is not a free adjustment.</h2><p>For each physical canonical pair, normalized plant coefficients satisfy αβ=t₀²/(LC). Their smallest possible common maximum is t₀/√(LC), achieved by balanced flux–charge scales. The inherited λ≤1 requirement therefore needs t₀≤min √(LC).</p><p>The timing allocation gives the other side: t₀ must be at least 400000 times the largest of output period, filter-history moment plus electronics latency, and actuator time constant. Both sides must hold on calibrated intervals.</p><p>The audited averaging-mode spacing yields a lower clock of 2048/5 seconds under this allocation. Every pair would then require LC≥4194304/25 seconds squared. A declared LC≤1 second squared envelope has no overlap. This is a restriction on the stated contract, not a universal impossibility result.</p></div></section>
 <section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>A filtered sample contains history.</h2><p>The readout theorem bounds error by state speed times the absolute first age moment of the filter, plus bounded raw error times the absolute coefficient sum. Signed filter weights can have zero signed group delay and still carry nonzero history error.</p><p>Filter mode, coefficients, aperture times, startup tails, channel skew and output availability must be recorded. Typical RMS noise and an advertised bit count do not establish the required simultaneous reconstruction bound.</p></div></section>
