+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Unified Dynamics public research atlas: finite tetrahedral mathematics, declared dynamics, source provenance and independently testable physical questions."><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f6ef"><title>Updates · Unified Dynamics</title><link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script src="/assets/app.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/atlas-hierarchy-v0.2.css"><script src="/assets/atlas-hierarchy-v0.2.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="editorial"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><aside class="sidebar"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 3L3 33H37Z M20 3L22 23L3 33 M22 23L37 33"/></svg><div>Unified<br>Dynamics<small>PUBLIC RESEARCH ATLAS</small></div></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation">Explore <span>☰</span></button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/"><span>01</span>Overview</a><a href="/primer/"><span>02</span>Foundations</a><a href="/research/"><span>03</span>Research programmes</a><a href="/evidence/"><span>04</span>Evidence & open questions</a><a href="/papers/"><span>05</span>Papers & reading</a><a href="/archive/" data-active="true"><span>06</span>Research archive</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><div class="atlas-utilities"><a href="/updates/">Updates</a><a href="/sources/">Search sources</a><a href="/contribution/">Contribute</a></div><span class="status-dot"></span>Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>AI-assisted research<br>Review remains scope-specific</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="atlas-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Overview</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/archive/">Research archive</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Dated updates</span></div><div class="atlas-context"><a href="/archive/">← Archive guide</a><span>Versioned source and research records</span></div><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Dated updates</div><h1>What changed,<br>and where to read it.</h1><p class="lede">Recent editorial and research notes. Current explanations live under Foundations and Research; original returns and older planning notes stay in the archive.</p></div><article class="claim" id="prefix-feasibility-v01"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 Chicago · Local prefix feasibility 0.1</div><h2>A proved local box admits a conditional plant/converter clock overlap</h2><p>Preparation-specific bounds reduce target sensitivity to 4 and speed to 8/5. The proposed controller admits 8/575&lt;t₀≤1/48 seconds; the example t₀=1/60 gives residual 0.00962. A named component route is screened with explicit leakage, noise, timing and actuator gates.</p><p>Five-page theorem; exact symbolic checks and twelve arithmetic controls pass. Hardware HOLD; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/prefix-feasibility/">Read the proof and component-return requirements →</a></article><article class="claim" id="clock-plant-v01"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 UTC · Clock–plant compatibility 0.1</div><h2>Converter selection now has a clock–plant compatibility gate</h2><p>The exact LC identity and a filtered-readout theorem prevent treating slower acquisition as a free remedy. A revised input/command allocation passes the prior Gauss budget, while hardware admission remains HOLD. Ten exact controls and inherited-budget replays pass.</p><p>Four-page theorem and preliminary supplier audit; identification plan OPEN; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/clock-plant/">Read theorem, audit and identification requirements →</a></article><article class="claim" id="sampled-actuator-v01"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 UTC · Sampled actuator certificate 0.1</div><h2>A controller and finite-bandwidth actuator fit the CD-1 prefix budget conditionally</h2><p>The theorem budgets sample age, input and command error, actuator lag, preload and range. The illustrative total residual is exactly 145267/16000000, below 1/100, preserving the prior short-window fault gaps. Nine exact controls and an inherited-budget replay pass.</p><p>Actual components, identification and calibration remain OPEN; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/sampled-actuator/">Read the theorem and instrument requirements →</a></article><article class="claim" id="finite-calibration-v01"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 UTC · Finite calibration certificate 0.1</div><h2>CD-1 now has a conditional finite-calibration and fault budget</h2><p>Explicit physical port commands, a finite-cover theorem and an exact short-window uncertainty allocation separate the nominal law from two specified faults near model time 1/100. Controller input age has its own bound, separate from readout spacing.</p><p>Six-page theorem manuscript; exact constructor checks. Apparatus bounds OPEN, independent review pending, physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/finite-calibration/">Read the certificate and apparatus-return requirements →</a></article><article class="claim" id="canonical-map-v01"><div class="eyebrow">20 October 2026 UTC · Canonical map and component test 0.1</div><h2>The apparatus map now fixes action, energy and time</h2><p>The scaled canonical theorem gives ordinary flux–charge pairs the required opposite matter brackets and reciprocal gauge dynamics. Sixty-four exact checks pass. CD-1 specifies one instrumented four-link diamond, independent coupling witnesses, known-source injection and two analytically discriminating fault controls.</p><p>Engineering gates are proposed and require actual calibration and non-constructor review. Physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/canonical-map/">Read theorem and closed-diamond test →</a></article><article class="claim" id="parity-review-packet-v01"><div class="eyebrow">19 October2026 · Review handoff v0.1</div><h2>The parity review target is now portable and hash-bound</h2><p>The unchanged prospective contract has an offline review packet,20-item brief and blank return form. Constructor replay and12 disclosed probes pass; they do not supply independent review or a new blind campaign.</p><a class="text-link" href="/research/parity/#parity-review-packet">Open the review handoff →</a></article><article class="claim" id="atlas-hierarchy-v02"><div class="eyebrow">19 October 2026 · Editorial reconciliation v0.2</div><h2>A reading hierarchy replaces the flat page list</h2><p>Six main destinations now separate foundations, research programmes, evidence, papers and the archive. Specialist pages keep their URLs; paper collections follow the argument, and capture logs are secondary to the programme summaries.</p><p>This changes presentation and navigation. Source statuses, scientific claim data and historical evidence remain preserved.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/atlas-structure-v0.2/EDITORIAL_RECONCILIATION.json">Editorial change record ↓</a></article><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Recent research publication.</h2><article class="claim" id="research-record-v01"><div class="eyebrow">19 October 2026 · Session publication v0.1</div><h2>Native parity, source custody and actual floating certificates</h2><p>The public record now indexes all 118 manifest-bearing captures and 122 events through repository snapshot 3064f129f7d5. Source-selected finite W, the typed parity repair and prospective contract join the source/receipt recovery and later preparation, timing, two-step noise and encoded projection/SVD records.</p><p>Historical protocols and source statuses stay preserved. Review remains scope-specific and pending/HOLD; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/research-record/">Read the session and inspect all captures →</a></article><article class="claim" id="measurable-audit-v01"><div class="eyebrow">18 October 2026 UTC · Dataset audit 0.1</div><h2>A nonlinear circuit candidate, with the data boundary explicit</h2><p>Selected a rank-one rotor–matter circuit emulator for development. Audited a complete published ten-channel waveform against its deposit checksum and inspected two cold-atom data sources. The published LC device is not the full UD Hamiltonian: its oscillator links differ from unitary rotor links, and its chain lacks the required diamonds.</p><p>No audited record supplies all preparation, energy and source-work gates. A closed-diamond component test and a calibrated measurement contract are the next experimental requirements. Constructor audit; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/measurable-audit/">Read selection, dataset audit and experiment requirements →</a></article></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>Earlier dated notes.</h2><details class="atlas-collection"><summary>Browse 39 earlier notes <span>Original order and wording retained</span></summary><article class="claim" id="source-work-v01"><div class="eyebrow">18 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The endpoint contract now includes controlled source and defect work</h2><p>A shared cumulative budget admits varying positive norms, other parameter drift and continuous tangent implementation errors. A graph ground-energy bound shows that pure base norm loss adds no positive reference-energy work, although it can still generate Gauss charge.</p><p>The seven-page theorem derives the covariant Gauss-source law, a drift envelope, raw matter-force allocation and a sufficient constant-rate duration inequality. An exact control shows why zero terminal net work cannot replace an every-prefix bound. Forty-three exact checks and twelve matrix diagnostics pass. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0. No actual numerical trajectory, physical source sector or experimental dataset is certified by this milestone.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#source-work-v01">Read the source and defect-work theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="energy-admission-v01"><div class="eyebrow">18 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>An energy gate extends sufficient preparation admission</h2><p>The new test combines the invariant geometry score with total actual orbit-relative energy. Conserved norm errors enter a shared centered-curvature allowance. An exact neutral electric circulation passes at 2.5 times the old sufficient initial radius.</p><p>The proof confines ambient Hamiltonian trajectories at nonzero Gauss levels without projecting them to zero. That does not make them solutions of the zero-source constrained action: unequal norms fail a strict trace condition. A square-root correction-distance example in the frozen cone prevents a linear residual correction claim there. The seven-page manuscript, exact calculator, 49 exact checks and matrix diagnostics are available. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0. The larger geometric ceiling requires the added energy test and is not an overall precision or basin-volume improvement.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#energy-admission-v01">Read the energy and residual theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="preparation-certificate-v01"><div class="eyebrow">18 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Preparation now has a gauge-invariant sufficient certificate</h2><p>A global orbit-distance upper score combines 27 real invariant inputs. Exact rational enclosures can establish entry into the previous endpoint trapping ball, with no prior local-chart assumption. Norm/Gauss validity, measurement coverage and model-error bounds remain separate inputs.</p><p>The eight-page theorem also proves an obstruction: configuration-only observations miss arbitrary admissible electric circulation, and active loop histories miss independent unused-color dynamics at rank ≥2. The exact calculator, 46 finite-topology/rational checks and numerical matrix controls are available. The conservative unit raw-interval allocation is about 2.93×10⁻⁴³ in model units, a sufficient bound rather than a physical precision requirement. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#preparation-certificate-v01">Read the preparation theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="observation-session-v01"><div class="eyebrow">17 October 2026 · Session publication</div><h2>600-cell observation, finite calibration and memory-limited spectra</h2><p>One 62-channel vertex link has ideal history rank 1893 and 747 hidden dimensions. A second link plus a tetrahedral port leaves 142; a separate 189-channel observer is complete for the declared nominal model. Finite conditioning and calibration certificates specify their own error assumptions.</p><p>The frozen 37-preparation record has 18,790,154 scalar readings. Verified Hankel actions, factor Grams and residual bounds avoid a 286.75-GiB dense allocation. Record-only and bounded-noise certificates preserve the distinction between retained threshold count and exact rank. All 26 accessible research packages/captures are now indexed in merged PR #179.</p><p>Independent review remains HOLD pending attributable, hash-bound corrected returns. Constructor verification is not experimental validation; physical promotion 0.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/observation/">Read the session results →</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD/pull/179">Merged evidence ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/users/ud-tetra/projects/1/views/1?pane=info&amp;statusUpdateId=289526">Project status ↗</a></div></article><article class="claim" id="endpoint-robustness-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The quartic endpoint now has explicit model-error allowances</h2><p>A cubic gradient budget covers generic Hamiltonian forces; centered errors with zero derivative on the nominal orbit use a quadratic curvature budget. Their shared inequality gives all-time static trapping and a compact minimizing invariant set with cube-root/square-root displacement bounds. A separate cumulative gradient-variation requirement handles time-dependent drift.</p><p>The eight-page theorem, exact calculator and mathematical requirements contract translate kinetic, magnetic, common/residual hopping and detuning errors into one budget. Loop readout limits include an instrument reserve and explicitly do not certify full preparation distance. An admitted stiffness increase shows why finite-radius trapping does not imply retained stability of the exact critical orbit. Twenty-four exact algebraic checks, five rational budget examples and four floating matrix diagnostics pass; frozen PET support remains conditional. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#endpoint-robustness-v01">Read the endpoint robustness theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="endpoint-parity-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The endpoint radius improves by at least 50,000</h2><p>The critical background has an isometric energy reflection that reverses the three soft directions and fixes their leading fast-relaxation correction. Three intrinsic tensor cancellations replace the earlier fifth-order loss by an explicit sixth-order bound. An exact polynomial